1a:I[28440,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6489","static/chunks/6489-699922ea493f9867.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","1356","static/chunks/1356-9e2757ba007ad1f9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","951","static/chunks/951-3dbda3a86d4c6444.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","2799","static/chunks/2799-ce430b82b11995d5.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6589","static/chunks/6589-cfdc115b042e146b.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","3650","static/chunks/3650-106e80ed926f59bf.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","8440","static/chunks/8440-bfb72547be677d52.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","134","static/chunks/app/%5Bpattern%5D/%5BproblemId%5D/page-f9e02a54f5641ad9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"AnimationRunner"]
19:["$","$L1a","serialize-deserialize",{"problem":{"id":"serialize-deserialize","patternId":"dfs","title":"Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","subtitle":"Pre-order with null markers, then rebuild from the stream","kind":"problem","leetcode":{"id":297,"slug":"serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree","difficulty":"Hard"},"companies":["Amazon","Meta","LinkedIn","Google"],"statement":"Design an algorithm to serialize a binary tree to a string and deserialize that string back into the identical tree. There is no restriction on the encoding/decoding format.","input":[],"render":"tree","approaches":[{"label":"Serialize · pre-order with null markers","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(n)"},"pseudocode":["serialize(node):","    if node: append(node.val)         // record on arrival (pre-order)","    else:    append(\"#\"); return      // null marker keeps it unambiguous","    serialize(node.left); serialize(node.right)","// result = \"1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#\""],"steps":[{"codeLine":1,"narration":"Serializing turns a tree into a flat string we can store or send. We walk PRE-ORDER (node, then left, then right) and write each value as we arrive. The trick that makes the string UNAMBIGUOUS: whenever a child is missing we still write a \"#\" marker. Without those null markers, \"1,2,3\" could rebuild into many different shapes, the #s pin down exactly where each subtree stops.","tree":{"root":{"id":"bt136","val":1,"left":{"id":"bt135","val":2,"left":null,"right":null},"right":{"id":"bt134","val":3,"left":{"id":"bt132","val":4,"left":null,"right":null},"right":{"id":"bt133","val":5,"left":null,"right":null}}},"activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":[],"notes":{},"noteColors":{},"stack":[],"label":"binary tree"},"vars":[["order","pre-order: node, L, R"],["serialized","∅"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Visit node 1. Pre-order means we record it the moment we arrive: append \"1\" to the string, then descend into its left subtree first, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt136","pathIds":["bt136"],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt136"]},"vars":[["visit",1],["append",1],["serialized","1"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Visit node 2. Pre-order means we record it the moment we arrive: append \"2\" to the string, then descend into its left subtree first, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt135","pathIds":["bt136","bt135"],"edgeHighlights":[["bt136","bt135"]],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(2)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt135"]},"vars":[["visit",2],["append",2],["serialized","1,2"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"left child of 2 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(2)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"right child of 2 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(2)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Visit node 3. Pre-order means we record it the moment we arrive: append \"3\" to the string, then descend into its left subtree first, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt134","pathIds":["bt136","bt134"],"edgeHighlights":[["bt136","bt134"]],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt134"]},"vars":[["visit",3],["append",3],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Visit node 4. Pre-order means we record it the moment we arrive: append \"4\" to the string, then descend into its left subtree first, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt132","pathIds":["bt136","bt134","bt132"],"edgeHighlights":[["bt136","bt134"],["bt134","bt132"]],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(4)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt132"]},"vars":[["visit",4],["append",4],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"left child of 4 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(4)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"right child of 4 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(4)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Visit node 5. Pre-order means we record it the moment we arrive: append \"5\" to the string, then descend into its left subtree first, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt133","pathIds":["bt136","bt134","bt133"],"edgeHighlights":[["bt136","bt134"],["bt134","bt133"]],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓","bt133":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green","bt133":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(5)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt133"]},"vars":[["visit",5],["append",5],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"left child of 5 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓","bt133":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green","bt133":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(5)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"right child of 5 is null. We don't skip it, we append \"#\". This marker is exactly what lets the reader know a subtree ENDS here, so the structure can be rebuilt without guessing.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓","bt133":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green","bt134":"green","bt132":"green","bt133":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"serialize(1)"},{"label":"serialize(3)"},{"label":"serialize(5)"}],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["visit","null"],["append","#"],["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"Pre-order traversal complete. The full encoding is \"1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#\". Read it back: 1, then its left subtree (2 with two null children), then its right subtree (3 with leaves 4 and 5). Every node and every gap is accounted for. We touch each node once and write a bounded number of tokens per node → O(n) time, and the string holds n values plus n+1 markers → O(n) space.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","pathIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"visitedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓","bt134":"✓","bt132":"✓","bt133":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"gold","bt135":"gold","bt134":"gold","bt132":"gold","bt133":"gold"},"stack":[],"label":"binary tree","highlightIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"]},"vars":[["serialized","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"],["time","O(n)"],["space","O(n)"]]}]},{"label":"Deserialize · rebuild from the stream","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(n)"},"pseudocode":["build():","    token = next()                    // consume left→right","    if token == \"#\": return null      // empty subtree","    node = new Node(token)","    node.left = build(); node.right = build()","    return node                       // first call returns the root"],"steps":[{"codeLine":1,"narration":"Deserializing reverses the process: given \"1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#\", rebuild the exact same tree. We read tokens strictly left→right with a cursor. The FIRST token is always the root; then, because the string was written pre-order, the very next tokens describe the whole LEFT subtree, and whatever's left describes the RIGHT.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":[],"notes":{},"noteColors":{},"stack":[],"dimmedIds":["bt136","bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"label":"rebuilding the tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["cursor",0],["remaining","1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Read \"1\"the root. Create the node, then recurse to build its left subtree before its right (the same order we wrote them in).","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt136","visitedIds":["bt136"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"build(1)"}],"dimmedIds":["bt135","bt134","bt132","bt133"],"label":"rebuilding the tree","highlightIds":["bt136"]},"vars":[["read","1"],["attach","root"],["cursor",1],["remaining","2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Read \"2\". Create a node and attach it as the left child of 1. Now recurse to fill in ITS left subtree, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt135","visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"build(1)"},{"label":"build(2)"}],"dimmedIds":["bt134","bt132","bt133"],"label":"rebuilding the tree","highlightIds":["bt135"]},"vars":[["read","2"],["attach","left of 1"],["cursor",2],["remaining","#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"Next token is \"#\". A \"#\" means \"no node here\", so the left child of 2 is null. We consume the marker and return immediately, nothing to attach. THIS is why the markers were essential: they tell us precisely when a branch is empty.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"build(1)"},{"label":"build(2)"}],"dimmedIds":["bt134","bt132","bt133"],"label":"rebuilding the tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["read","#"],["→","null"],["cursor",3],["remaining","#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"Next token is \"#\". A \"#\" means \"no node here\", so the right child of 2 is null. We consume the marker and return immediately, nothing to attach. THIS is why the markers were essential: they tell us precisely when a branch is empty.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"$undefined","visitedIds":["bt136","bt135"],"notes":{"bt136":"✓","bt135":"✓"},"noteColors":{"bt136":"green","bt135":"green"},"stack":[{"label":"build(1)"},{"label":"build(2)"}],"dimmedIds":["bt134","bt132","bt133"],"label":"rebuilding the tree","highlightIds":[]},"vars":[["read","#"],["→","null"],["cursor",4],["remaining","3,4,#,#,5,#,#"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"Read \"3\". Create a node and attach it as the right child of 1. Now recurse to fill in ITS left subtree, then its right.","tree":{"root":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:tree:root","activeId":"bt134","visitedIds"